1. Salesforce — Lightning Web Components | ~55 min

An LWC usually has three core files:

accountSearch/
│
├── accountSearch.html
├── accountSearch.js
└── accountSearch.js-meta.xml

Often you'll also have:

accountSearch.css

Think of them as:

HTML → UI
JS   → behaviour/state
XML  → where Salesforce can expose the component
CSS  → component styling

A simple component:

<template>
    <lightning-card title="Welcome">
        <div class="slds-p-around_medium">
            Hello {userName}
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>

import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

export default class WelcomeComponent extends LightningElement {
    userName = 'Rachel';
}

When userName changes, LWC's reactive rendering updates the relevant UI.

LWC lifecycle

Know these hooks:

constructor()
      ↓
connectedCallback()
      ↓
render()
      ↓
renderedCallback()

component removed
      ↓
disconnectedCallback()

constructor() is for initial component setup. Don't try accessing rendered DOM elements here because the template hasn't been inserted yet.

connectedCallback() runs when the component is inserted into the DOM. Useful for initialization that depends on the component being connected.

connectedCallback() {
    console.log('Component connected');
}

renderedCallback() executes after rendering and may execute multiple times.

This is a classic interview trap:

renderedCallback() {
    this.someReactiveProperty++;
}

Reactive state change → rerender → renderedCallback() → state change → rerender...

You can accidentally create a loop.

disconnectedCallback() is useful for cleanup such as event subscriptions/listeners.

@api

Used to expose a public property or method to a parent component.

Child:

import {
    LightningElement,
    api
} from 'lwc';

export default class AccountDetails
    extends LightningElement {

    @api recordId;
}

Parent:

<c-account-details
    record-id={selectedAccountId}>
</c-account-details>

Mental model:

Parent
   │
   │ @api property
   ↓
Child

@wire

Suppose Apex:

public with sharing class AccountController {

    @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
    public static List<Account> getAccounts() {

        return [
            SELECT Id, Name, Industry
            FROM Account
            LIMIT 20
        ];
    }
}

LWC:

import { LightningElement, wire }
    from 'lwc';

import getAccounts
    from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.getAccounts';

export default class AccountList
    extends LightningElement {

    @wire(getAccounts)
    accounts;
}

You can access:

accounts.data
accounts.error

Another common form:

@wire(getAccounts)
wiredAccounts({ data, error }) {

    if (data) {
        this.accounts = data;
        this.error = undefined;
    }

    if (error) {
        this.error = error;
        this.accounts = undefined;
    }
}

Reactive wire parameters

Apex:

@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
public static List<Account> getAccounts(
    String industry
) {

    return [
        SELECT Id, Name, Industry
        FROM Account
        WHERE Industry = :industry
    ];
}

JS:

industry = 'Banking';

@wire(getAccounts, {
    industry: '$industry'
})
accounts;

The $ is important.

industry: '$industry'

means the parameter is reactive.

When industry changes, the wire can provision new data.

@wire vs imperative Apex

Imperative:

async handleSearch() {

    try {

        const result =
            await getAccounts({
                industry: this.industry
            });

        this.accounts = result;

    } catch (error) {

        this.error = error;

    }
}

A useful interview distinction:

Wire fits reactive/declarative data provisioning, especially read scenarios.

Imperative gives you explicit control over when Apex executes and is commonly used for button-driven actions, DML methods, or procedural flows.

Don't say:

> @wire = GET and imperative = POST.



That's not the right abstraction.

Why cacheable=true?

@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)

tells the framework the Apex method is read-only/cacheable.

Therefore this is wrong:

@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
public static void updateAccount(Account acc) {
    update acc;
}

A cacheable Apex method shouldn't perform data mutation.

LWC → Apex → Database

Architecture:

LWC
 │
 │ @wire / imperative
 ↓
Apex Controller
 │
 │ SOQL / DML
 ↓
Salesforce Database

But there's an important security problem.

Consider:

public class AccountController {

    @AuraEnabled
    public static List<Account> getAccounts() {

        return [
            SELECT Id,
                   Name,
                   AnnualRevenue
            FROM Account
        ];
    }
}

Apex doesn't automatically guarantee every aspect of object/field access just because the user called it from an LWC.

We'll do a full security session later, but start building the habit of thinking about:

Sharing
CRUD
FLS

For example:

public with sharing class AccountController {

with sharing helps enforce record-level sharing rules.

But remember:

> with sharing does not by itself enforce CRUD/FLS.



That's a favourite interview question.

Parent → Child communication

Parent:

<c-account-details
    account-id={selectedAccountId}>
