Lightning Message Service

Now suppose components aren't in a simple parent-child hierarchy.

Example:

Component A

      unrelated

Component B

For cross-DOM/component communication, Lightning Message Service (LMS) is a standard option.

Architecture:

Component A
    ↓
Message Channel
    ↓
Component B

Interviewers may ask:

> How would two unrelated LWCs communicate?



A good answer:

> If they don't have a direct parent-child relationship, I'd consider Lightning Message Service using a Lightning Message Channel. For direct parent-child communication I'd prefer public APIs and custom events instead of introducing LMS unnecessarily.



That's a much better answer than “use pub-sub everywhere.”

LWC interview drill

Be ready to answer:

1. What is LWC?


2. @api vs @wire?


3. Wire vs imperative Apex?


4. Why use $property in wire parameters?


5. What does cacheable=true mean?


6. Can a cacheable Apex method perform DML?


7. Parent → child communication?


8. Child → parent communication?


9. How do unrelated LWCs communicate?


10. connectedCallback() vs renderedCallback()?


11. Why can renderedCallback() cause an infinite loop?


12. Does with sharing enforce FLS?



For:

> When would you use imperative Apex instead of @wire?



A good interview answer is:

> I use imperative Apex when execution should happen explicitly, such as after a button click, when performing data mutations, or when I need procedural control over request handling. I use wire when the data requirement is reactive and fits the wire service's declarative model.



2. Core Java — Interface vs Abstract Class | ~25 min

You've already covered basic OOP. Now let's make it interview-ready.

Interface:

interface PaymentProcessor {

    void processPayment(double amount);
}

Implementation:

class CreditCardProcessor
        implements PaymentProcessor {

    @Override
    public void processPayment(
        double amount
    ) {

        System.out.println(
            "Card payment: " + amount
        );
    }
}

Another:

class UpiProcessor
        implements PaymentProcessor {

    @Override
    public void processPayment(
        double amount
    ) {

        System.out.println(
            "UPI payment: " + amount
        );
    }
}

Now:

PaymentProcessor processor =
    new UpiProcessor();

processor.processPayment(1000);

The calling code depends on the contract, not a concrete implementation.

That's abstraction and polymorphism working together.

Abstract class

abstract class Employee {

    protected String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    abstract void work();

    void login() {
        System.out.println(
            name + " logged in"
        );
    }
}

Child:

class Developer extends Employee {

    Developer(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    @Override
    void work() {
        System.out.println(
            "Writing code"
        );
    }
}

The abstract class can hold state, constructors and concrete implementation.

Interview comparison

Think:

Interface
→ contract/capability

Abstract class
→ shared base abstraction + state/behaviour

Modern Java interfaces can also have default and static methods, so avoid outdated answers like:

> “Interfaces can never contain implemented methods.”



Multiple interfaces

Java doesn't allow a class to extend multiple classes:

class C extends A, B

❌

But:

class PaymentService
    implements
        PaymentProcessor,
        Auditable {
}

is valid.

This gives Java a way to compose multiple behavioural contracts without multiple class inheritance.

